  "id": "ch6-tactics-and-plans",
  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "intro": "Six positions, each ending on the move that decides it. They are the patterns this opening produces over and over, so learning them is worth more than learning another twenty moves of a line.\n\nWork through them as exercises: play to the position, then find White's move before you look.\n\n## The patterns\n\n**The e5 wedge.** Taking on e5 hits the knight on f6, and a knight that has to move is a tempo. Half the material this course wins comes from that one fact.\n\n**Blocking a check with a piece, not a pawn.** After ...Qh4+ the answer is almost never g3. It is Nf2, Ng3 or Ke2, depending on the position, because g3 opens the very lines Black checked to open.\n\n**Nd5 and the discovered check.** With the white queen on e2 and a black king on e8, exd6 comes with check. That is what turns a two-move fork into a winning attack.\n\n**The undefended knight.** ...Ne4 and ...Ng4 are the two squares Black jumps to when he has nothing. Neither is defended, and Nxe4 or Qxg4 answers both.\n\n**Giving the pawn back.** When a piece up, the right move is usually the one that hands back a pawn to finish developing. exd6 in exercise five is the model.\n\n**The raid on g7.** After ...Bxc3 and dxc3 the queen goes to g4, and Black cannot hold both e4 and g7. The recapture with the d-pawn is what makes it work, because it opens the c1 bishop for Bg5 or Bh6.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nBlocking with g3 out of habit. Taking with the knight on g5 and then castling long, when the queen should recapture with check. Reaching for the attack when there is a piece hanging. And, once a piece up, hunting for a second one instead of trading down.",
  "defaultTags": ["tactics"],
  "stem": ["e4", "e5", "Nc3", "Nf6", "f4"],
